Enter Your Pin

A while loop and an if statement both check to see if a condition is true
An if statement only checks a condition once while a while loop keeps checking the condition until it is false.
All the inputs would have to have an int() in front of them.
 
PIN = "12345"

print("WELCOME TO THE BANK OF GALLO.")
entry = input("ENTER YOUR PIN: ")
while entry != PIN:
    print("\nINCORRECT PIN. TRY AGAIN.")
    #entry = input("ENTER YOUR PIN: ")
print("\nPIN ACCEPTED. YOU NOW HAVE ACCESS TO YOUR ACCOUNT.")

If the pin is incorrect, Python will keep printing “Incorrect pin.Try Again, as entry will forever be not equal to pin.


Keep Guessing
import random
secretNumber=random.randrange(1,11)
guess=int(input("Guess a number between 1 and 10: "))
while guess!=secretNumber:
   print("Wrong try again")
   guess=int(input("Guess a number between 1 and 10: "))
else:
   print("correct")

